# cliphist — how much clipboard history is kept.
#
# The copies themselves are already SNAPSHOTS: cliphist reads the bytes at copy
# time, so an entry survives the app that made it closing, the chat or document
# being deleted, or the selection being cleared. Verified 2026-11-04 on this
# machine — copied a marker, killed the owning client so the clipboard read
# "Nothing is copied", and the entry was still listed and still decoded.
#
# The only thing that ever removed an entry was the store's cap. The default is
# 750, and the store sat permanently at it: 750 rows with the newest id 3120,
# i.e. 2370 copies already dropped. A copy you meant to keep silently ages out
# as newer ones arrive.
#
# 100, on request (2026-11-16). The cap is enforced by cliphist itself on every
# store, so the hundred-and-first copy deletes the oldest one by itself -- no
# timer, no script of ours in the path, and a copy made by hand with `cliphist
# store` obeys it too. The old 2000 was chosen for retention and settled near
# 150 MB on disk (images dominate); 100 is a small fraction of that and, at the
# rate this machine copies, about a day of history.
#
# The cost of the cap is that a PIN does not outlive it: pins are matched by
# content (see ClipboardListWindow/Cliphist.qml), and once the cap deletes an
# entry its content is gone from the store, so the pinned section drops it.
# Re-pin it, or copy it again, to bring it back to the top of the hundred.
#
# Format: one "<key> <value>" per line, read by every cliphist call (the
# wl-paste watchers in hyprland/execs.lua pass no flags, so they inherit this).
max-items 100
